WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.990
- Well, let's talk about your movie.

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:01.823
I loved your movie.

00:00:01.823 --> 00:00:02.831
- Thank you!

00:00:02.831 --> 00:00:03.664
- You're very witty.

00:00:03.664 --> 00:00:04.497
I was like, yeah.

00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:05.700
You and Roy, your co-star, as well.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.380
I love...it's so witty.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.270
And it's kind of...

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.560
Okay, it's called "Confess, Fletch",

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.840
and, would you describe it
as kind of like a whodunit?

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.720
- Yes, it's based on the
movie from the eighties,

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.615
that some of you,

00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:18.810
if I'm looking out and
judging ages correctly, you-

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.040
- So, I didn't know.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.865
- May actually remember
this, you- (laughs)

00:00:23.865 --> 00:00:25.020
- (laughs)

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:25.853
- I didn't actually know!

00:00:25.853 --> 00:00:27.420
- The rest, not at all.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.763
No, it's based on a
film from the eighties,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:31.950
starring Chevy Chase,

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:32.783
- Love.

00:00:32.783 --> 00:00:35.520
- But it's really based
on this series of novels

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.020
that were written by a
guy named Jeffrey McDonald

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.100
in the seventies and the eighties.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.380
And it's based on a detective,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.020
or, actually, an investigative reporter.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:43.853
- Yeah.

00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:44.940
- And the author of the novels himself

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.250
was an investigative reporter
for the Boston Globe,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.350
and he kind of took his experiences

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:50.670
and rolled them into this character.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.280
I.M., Irwin Maurice Fletcher.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.320
Famously made popular by Chevy,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.960
but reimagined for us
for the 21st century.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.990
And we have this whole backlog
of books to go through,

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:01.860
and they're all stories,

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.620
and there's a lot of
high jinks in whodunits,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.940
and it's just a fun story.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.770
I mean, there's a reason why people like

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.180
to read mystery novels.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.023
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:10.023 --> 00:01:11.520
- Because they have a beginning,

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.507
a middle, and an end, and--

00:01:12.507 --> 00:01:15.065
- And the cast of characters,
and all their personalities.

00:01:15.065 --> 00:01:15.898
- It's a fun ride, and you know,

00:01:15.898 --> 00:01:17.040
you started to see it with...

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.670
Maybe some people like "Only
Murders in the Building",

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.260
or "Knives Out", or--

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.188
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:23.188 --> 00:01:25.290
These things that have been really kind of

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.030
sparked in the culture recently.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:27.863
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:27.863 --> 00:01:28.696
- And I think that that's

00:01:28.696 --> 00:01:29.850
kind of where we are with this film,

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.190
and we're excited that
it's gonna be the beginning

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.260
of, hopefully, a franchise,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.490
that I can keep playing this guy,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.379
because I need job security.

00:01:37.379 --> 00:01:39.870
- (laughs) I loved it though.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.570
I'm not...I never was into whodunit

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.400
until somebody had me, like,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.420
I just asked what somebody was reading

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.370
'cause they were, like, really into it.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:47.203
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:47.203 --> 00:01:48.618
- And it ended up being Agatha Christie.

00:01:48.618 --> 00:01:49.451
- [Jon] Oh, yeah.

00:01:49.451 --> 00:01:50.284
- And I was like...well,

00:01:50.284 --> 00:01:51.330
I didn't know anything
about Agatha Christie,

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:53.340
and so I...I just missed it, I don't know.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.810
And I'd never been into whodunit,

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.400
and it got me into whodunit.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.810
And I loved the show for that,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.227
because the whole time
you're kind of, like,

00:01:59.227 --> 00:02:00.526
"Wow, is it the weird neighbor?"

00:02:00.526 --> 00:02:01.359
- Well, Kenneth Branagh just made

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:02.299
"Murder on the Orient Express"
and "Death on the Nile".

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:03.132
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:03.132 --> 00:02:04.020
- Like, so those are a similar thing.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.790
Like, I feel like there's
something in the culture

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.680
where we're all excited to have

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.340
any kind of certainty or something...

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.350
a situation where it gets resolved.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.183
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:14.183 --> 00:02:15.016
- It feels like everything right now

00:02:15.016 --> 00:02:15.990
in the world is unresolved,

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:18.719
and it it's nice to watch
a story that gets resolved.

00:02:18.719 --> 00:02:19.552
- [Kelly] What?

00:02:19.552 --> 00:02:20.385
What are you talking about?

00:02:20.385 --> 00:02:21.218
What are you referring to?

00:02:21.218 --> 00:02:22.051
- I can't imagine any examples.

00:02:22.051 --> 00:02:24.997
- No, but I think what's
interesting about this project is,

00:02:24.997 --> 00:02:25.890
"Confess, Fletch" is...

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.650
it's got that, but, the
level of comedic timing,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.570
like, the humor is so funny!

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.550
And that's not generally what I would find

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:33.383
in Agatha Christie or something like that.

00:02:33.383 --> 00:02:34.216
- Right.

00:02:34.216 --> 00:02:35.130
- So, I liked that.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:36.900
I think the humor element is awesome.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.360
- It's definitely very
contemporary in its humor.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.193
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:42.480
- And we have an amazing
cast in this film.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.430
Not only Roy, who'll be
here later, but we have

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.453
Marcia Gay Harden,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.210
Annie Mumolo, who plays
the neighbor next door,

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.800
who you might remember from "Bridesmaids"

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.870
and "Barb and Star Go to Vista Del Mar".

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:52.740
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.180
- We have John Slattery,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:56.037
my castmate from "Mad Men",

00:02:57.641 --> 00:02:58.474
- [Kelly] It's so good.

00:02:58.474 --> 00:03:00.060
- Ayden Mayeri, we have amazing
people all the way through.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.530
We shot it in Boston and
environs around Massachusetts,

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:05.363
so, it was lovely.

00:03:05.363 --> 00:03:06.433
- [Kelly] It's a great film.

00:03:06.433 --> 00:03:07.266
- Thanks!

00:03:07.266 --> 00:03:08.099
- I love, I hope that it's more of, like,

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:08.932
a series, and you could...there's more.

00:03:08.932 --> 00:03:09.765
That's cool.

00:03:09.765 --> 00:03:10.598
I love the character.

00:03:10.598 --> 00:03:11.431
- Well, I hope to come
and sit back on this couch

00:03:11.431 --> 00:03:12.264
and tell you all about the sequels.

00:03:12.264 --> 00:03:13.097
- Okay, fine!

00:03:13.097 --> 00:03:15.870
Well, we have your co-star, Roy Wood Jr.,

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:16.710
he's joining us next,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:17.640
was that fun to work with him?

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.900
You two are funny.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.050
- Roy is, first of all,
he's a phenomenal comedian,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.000
and his work on "The
Daily Show" is amazing.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.350
He's a wildly intelligent guy.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:29.460
But part of what Roy brought
to the table was, really,

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.370
believability in this kind of...

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.300
being a legitimate antagonist
for the Fletch character.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.305
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:37.305 --> 00:03:38.138
- And actually thinking, like,

00:03:38.138 --> 00:03:39.480
"Well, he has to be a believable cop.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.300
He can't just be some kind
of joke delivery machine."

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.133
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:47.250
- And Roy has a gravitas to
him that is nicely offset

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.020
by his tremendous sense of humor.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.580
- Yeah. He did a great
job of being serious.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.140
And like, you can actually
believe that he was a cop,

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.372
but he was very humorous.

00:03:53.372 --> 00:03:54.205
- There's a point in the movie where

00:03:54.205 --> 00:03:55.447
he slams me up against
a car, where I was like,

00:03:55.447 --> 00:03:57.244
"Oh, you were really,
yeah, okay, that...yep!"

00:03:57.244 --> 00:03:58.420
- [Kelly] "This is real!"

00:03:58.420 --> 00:03:59.550
- "You, you could be a cop."

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:03.201
- Please welcome Roy Wood Jr., everybody!

00:04:03.201 --> 00:04:05.620
(funky music)

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.537
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:04:23.036 --> 00:04:24.369
- How you doing?

00:04:26.864 --> 00:04:27.810
Good to see you, brother.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:28.830
- Wait, I had to ask.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.330
'Cause y'all, I feel like y'all

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.710
had to have known each other forever.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.960
Your comedic timing together
is so brilliant on screen.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.120
So how long, like, when
did y'all first meet?

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.078
- In makeup.

00:04:37.078 --> 00:04:38.040
(Jon laughs)

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.840
- No! You didn't know
each other before this?

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:41.580
- I'd seen him around comedy clubs, like,

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:42.413
he's a fan of comedy.

00:04:42.413 --> 00:04:43.248
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:43.248 --> 00:04:45.360
- So Jon Hamm shows up at
The Comedy Cellar in New York

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.791
all the time, but you don't just go over,

00:04:46.791 --> 00:04:49.850
(in a silly voice) "Hey, Jon
Hamm, do you like comedy?"

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:50.730
(laughter)

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:51.930
- Especially like that.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.420
- As a comedian, you know, when
you're dealing with someone

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.490
that is on that level comedically,

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:59.460
and it was just, it was fun from the jump.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.293
I'm just thankful.

00:05:00.293 --> 00:05:01.126
- That's remarkable though,

00:05:01.126 --> 00:05:02.400
because y'all do so well together

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.080
feeding off of each other,
and, like, your timing.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.670
That's funny that you'd
never met each other.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:06.690
I wouldn't have guessed that.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.360
- No, it was really, really cool.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.280
- [Kelly] Well, what
was the audition like?

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.720
- It was on Zoom.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.650
It was Zoom.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.450
- Well, that helps you connect.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.130
- I don't know if you knew this,

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:20.430
I turned everybody's camera
off when I did the audition.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.940
Because, like, on Zoom, it's
you and Jon Hamm reading,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.730
but then, there's, like,
other people watching,

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:28.410
and, I don't know y'all!

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.517
Block, block, block,
block, block, block, block.

00:05:31.517 --> 00:05:32.350
(audience laughs)

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:33.810
Because the, the intimidation of just,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:37.200
number one, trying to do the
role justice, number one,

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.940
and you're rebooting something

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.370
that's a very celebrated franchise.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.410
So, you don't want be the
person that messes it up.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:44.305
You want to get it right.

00:05:44.305 --> 00:05:45.138
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:45.138 --> 00:05:46.650
- And, so, I can't get it right

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.120
with all of these extra eyeballs.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.290
- That's intimidating.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.120
- It's like meeting a girl's
family at the holidays.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.560
It was, like, all these
extra family members,

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:54.427
I'm just here to meet Jon Hamm.

00:05:54.427 --> 00:05:55.260
- Yeah.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:56.093
"Why are you here?"

00:05:56.093 --> 00:05:57.420
- Yeah. Who are all these other people?

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.333
- Wait, so what do you
remember from his audition?

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.660
- It's, you know, like, as Roy said,

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.737
anytime you're trying to establish

00:06:05.737 --> 00:06:08.610
"Well, will there be chemistry
between these two people?"

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:12.123
over zoom, looking at
a screen with a delay,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:14.700
it's nearly impossible,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:18.390
but I knew as soon as we
got through the first scene,

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.500
I was like, "Well, Roy's got it."

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.630
'Cause, as I said, Roy's got a,

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.720
first of all, an established,
unbelievably wonderful

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:25.553
sense of humor,

00:06:25.553 --> 00:06:27.660
but, a believable...he's a big guy,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.210
and he is a believable person who is

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.800
a member of law enforcement.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:32.670
- Yeah.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.560
- And a guy that you definitely

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.390
don't want to be on the wrong side of.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.262
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:37.262 --> 00:06:38.095
- Even though he is very funny.

00:06:38.095 --> 00:06:39.783
So, we needed both of those things,

00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:44.310
and Roy was able to deliver,
you know, times a million.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:45.750
- Yeah, oh, it's so good.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.988
Wait, so in "Confess,
Fletch", you play a cop.

00:06:47.988 --> 00:06:50.070
So is it true that you
actually have family members

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:50.903
that are cops?

00:06:50.903 --> 00:06:51.736
Did they try and give you advice?

00:06:51.736 --> 00:06:54.250
- Yeah. They're they're
they're young cops though.

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:55.560
- Well, what does that mean?

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:56.520
They're still real cops.

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.230
- Well, no, but in the movie

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:01.230
I'm a grizzled veteran detective.

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:02.063
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:07:02.063 --> 00:07:03.027
- The cops in my family, they're like,

00:07:03.027 --> 00:07:04.890
28, 29, you know?

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:05.940
They still got energy,

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.462
and they found out I was getting the role,

00:07:08.462 --> 00:07:09.295
I didn't tell you this.

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:10.128
They're like, "Oh, well,

00:07:10.128 --> 00:07:13.311
let me know if you need
me on set as a consultant,

00:07:13.311 --> 00:07:14.905
you know, if you need to learn something."

00:07:14.905 --> 00:07:16.110
- [Jon] Let me get that
free trip to Boston.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.107
- Yeah.

00:07:17.107 --> 00:07:18.630
"Well, you know you might need to learn

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:20.550
some weapons training, what kind of gun

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:21.870
does your character use?

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.240
Or maybe some car chasing,

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.250
I could teach you defensive driving."

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:27.083
Hey man.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:32.013
I'm a cop with a newborn baby
chasing a barefoot white man.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.280
That's all I'm doing in the movie.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.540
- It's not that deep.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.183
- If I get cast in "Bad
Boys 4" I'll let you know.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:41.880
- Thank you for your advice.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.100
- Yeah. We can do all
the hand-to-hand combat.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:44.933
- I love that.

00:07:44.933 --> 00:07:47.160
So, wait, how do you
guys think that you'd do

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:48.330
solving a real life murder?

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.220
Are you good at stuff like that?

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:51.113
Like, guessing things?

00:07:51.113 --> 00:07:53.040
- I mean, I have, I've made it through

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.570
50, some odd years on this planet.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:56.070
I think I'm relatively intuitive.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:57.960
I don't know if I'd want anybody

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.450
depending on me to solve anything.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:01.290
- I'd be good.

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:02.123
- Okay.

00:08:02.123 --> 00:08:02.956
(audience laughs)

00:08:02.956 --> 00:08:04.347
- I love it, he's, like,
kinda humble, he's like

00:08:04.347 --> 00:08:05.180
"Oh, I'd nail it."

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:06.750
- All you have to do to be a detective

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.047
is just send stuff to the lab.

00:08:08.047 --> 00:08:10.350
(audience laughs)

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.183
That's all they do.

00:08:11.183 --> 00:08:12.250
They stare at something...

00:08:16.117 --> 00:08:17.220
"There it is."

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:18.330
- [Kelly] There it is.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.163
- Get that back to the lab.

00:08:19.163 --> 00:08:20.203
- Send that to the lab.

00:08:20.203 --> 00:08:21.036
(audience laughs)

00:08:21.036 --> 00:08:21.869
- And that's it.

00:08:21.869 --> 00:08:22.702
- And you just solved the case.

